From: Helldez <74499579+Helldez@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Apr 2026 12:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] fix(parakeet): emit open VAD segment so mid-segment
 partials can fire

The mid-segment partial decode branch added in 0002 was structurally
unreachable on the OnePlus CPH2769 (and likely any device with a normal
speaking pace). Diagnosis from a 63 s dictation session:

- 14 final segments were emitted, all with `isPartial=false`.
- Several spans of >9 s of continuous speech yielded zero partials,
  even though `partial_decode_interval_ms=1500` was correctly forwarded
  through the JS config and the addon's `partialDecodeIntervalSamples`.

Root cause is in 0001's `SileroVad::getSegments()`: the function only
returns segments that have already been **closed** by silence detection
(when `silenceLen >= minSilenceFrames`) or by the hard `maxSpeechFrames`
cap. While the user is still speaking and no terminal silence has been
observed, the trailing in-speech run is silently discarded:

    // Trailing speech that never closed — leave open for the next
    // invocation unless the caller is at end of stream
    return segments;   // ← returns an EMPTY vector during ongoing speech

Patch 0002's partial-decode block was conditioned on
`!segments.empty() && lastComplete < 0`, which is `false && false`
during the very window the partial decode is supposed to cover. The
branch never executes. The reference implementation we audited
(HearoPilot's sherpa-onnx integration) sidesteps this by tracking
`vad.isSpeechDetected()` directly instead of waiting for closed
segments to appear in the queue.

Fix: append a *preliminary* (open) segment to the return value when
`inSpeech` is still true at the end of the probability scan. Its `t1Cs`
is set to the last processed frame (≈ buffer end), which makes patch
0002's existing `segmentStillOpen = lastT1S + marginS >= liveDurationS`
check evaluate to true, and `lastComplete` stays at `-1` because no
*final* commit happened this pass. The partial-decode branch then runs
as designed — Parakeet is re-run on the in-progress range and emits
`isPartial=true`.

The preliminary segment is recomputed from scratch on every
`getSegments()` call (each call begins with a `reset()` of the LSTM
state and re-scans the buffer), so there is no cross-call state to
maintain. When VAD eventually observes the closing silence, that pass
returns the *closed* segment in the normal way, the for-loop in 0002
processes it as final, the buffer is trimmed, and the next iteration
starts fresh with no preliminary until speech resumes.

Behaviour summary:
- Continuous speech: preliminary segment present every pass; partial
  cadence drives mid-segment isPartial=true emissions every
  `partial_decode_interval_ms` of new audio (1.5 s default).
- End of speech: closed segment emitted as final; preliminary absent
  on the same pass; no double-emission.
- Pause then resume mid-utterance: pause ≥ minSilenceDurationMs closes
  the segment as final and trims; resumed speech opens a new
  preliminary on the trimmed buffer; cadence restarts cleanly.
- Cap at `maxSpeechDurationS`: the existing closed-segment path still
  fires; preliminary covers any residual tail.

Smallest possible change — does not touch SileroVad's public surface,
StreamingProcessor, or the addon JS bridge. Backwards-compatible: a
caller that only loops over `segments` to read finals sees no change
because, in their normal use, the preliminary at the end will be
re-reported as a closed segment on the next pass once silence lands,
and they were always tolerant of "extra" segments showing up across
calls (StreamingProcessor only acts on segments that satisfy the
`lastT1S + marginS < liveDurationS` predicate).
---
 .../addon/src/model-interface/SileroVad.cpp   | 18 +++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/packages/qvac-lib-infer-parakeet/addon/src/model-interface/SileroVad.cpp b/packages/qvac-lib-infer-parakeet/addon/src/model-interface/SileroVad.cpp
index 0000000..0000001 100644
--- a/packages/qvac-lib-infer-parakeet/addon/src/model-interface/SileroVad.cpp
+++ b/packages/qvac-lib-infer-parakeet/addon/src/model-interface/SileroVad.cpp
@@ -496,9 +496,25 @@ std::vector<SileroVad::Segment> SileroVad::getSegments(
     }
   }

-  // Trailing speech that never closed — leave open for the next invocation
-  // unless the caller is at end of stream (handled at the
-  // StreamingProcessor level via the "force-process final buffer" path).
+  // Trailing speech that never closed: emit a *preliminary* (open) segment
+  // covering [speechStartFrame, numWindows-1] so the StreamingProcessor's
+  // mid-segment partial-decode branch (patch 0002) has a non-empty queue
+  // and a `lastT1S` that satisfies `segmentStillOpen`. The loop above will
+  // emit a *closed* segment in its place on the pass where silence is
+  // finally observed; that closed segment is what triggers the final
+  // commit + buffer trim.
+  //
+  // Without this, getSegments() returns an empty vector during ongoing
+  // speech, the partial branch's `!segments.empty()` check is never true,
+  // and the recognizer is run only at VAD endpoints — i.e. never as a
+  // partial. End-of-stream (force-process final buffer) is unaffected
+  // because StreamingProcessor handles that path on the JS side via
+  // endStreaming() and does not consult this segment list.
+  if (inSpeech) {
+    const int speechEndFrame = std::max(speechStartFrame + 1, numWindows - 1);
+    segments.push_back(
+        {framesToCs(speechStartFrame), framesToCs(speechEndFrame)});
+  }
   return segments;
 }

